    answer: 'When obstructive events persist at high CPAP pressures (15 cm H2O), the patient should be transitioned to bilevel PAP. The recommended starting point is an IPAP of 17 cm H2O and EPAP of 13 cm H2O (maintaining the therapeutic expiratory pressure while adding inspiratory support). IPAP is then titrated upward in 1 cm H2O increments to resolve remaining hypopneas and desaturations. The pressure support (IPAP minus EPAP) improves tidal volume, which addresses the residual hypopneas. Minimum pressure support is typically 4 cm H2O, and maximum IPAP is generally 25-30 cm H2O.',
